</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">BD3-LMs achieve the best generative perplexities compared to previous diffusion methods. Relative to SSD-LM, our discrete approach yields samples with improved generative perplexity using an order of magnitude fewer generation steps. We also qualitatively examine samples taken from BD3-LM and baselines (AR, MDLM) trained on the OWT dataset; we report samples in Suppl. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Samples ‣ Block Diffusion: Interpolating Between Autoregressive and Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. We observe that BD3-LM samples have higher coherence than MDLM samples and approach the quality of AR.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Ablations</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess the impact of the design choices in our proposed block diffusion recipes, namely 1) selection of the noise schedule and 2) the efficiency improvement of the proposed training algorithm relative to a naive implementation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Selecting Noise Schedules to Reduce Training Variance</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the linear schedule used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, training under “clipped” noise schedules is the most effective for reducing the training variance which correlates with test perplexity. In Table <a href="#S6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Selecting Noise Schedules to Reduce Training Variance ‣ 6.3 Ablations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Block Diffusion: Interpolating Between Autoregressive and Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the ideal “clipped” masking rates, which are optimized during training, are specific to the block size and further motivate our optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T8" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:42%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Effect of the noise schedule on likelihood estimation. We finetune BD3-LMs on 3B tokens from LM1B and evaluate on a linear schedule. For clipped schedules, we compare optimal clipping for <math id="S6.T8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}=4,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}=4,16</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
