<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>We can adapt the masking strategy from <a href="#A2.F3" title="In B.6 Specialized Attention Masks ‣ Appendix B Masked BD3-LMs ‣ Block Diffusion: Interpolating Between Autoregressive and Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to a FlexAttention compatible sparse masking function as above. This enables the creation of a customized JIT attention operation that uses significantly less memory with up to <math id="A2.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>5X speedup over the naive native scaled_dot_product_attention implementation in PyTorch (<math id="A2.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> 2.5) on a A5000 GPU with <math id="A2.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1024</annotation></semantics></math> and batch size <math id="A2.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=16</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
